  "prompt": "I have 3 completely independent work streams to run in parallel. NOTHING is given to you directly — every value and identifier lives in the system's records and must be resolved by following references from one record to the next. For each item, start from the named worklist and dereference step by step until you reach the concrete value, then act. (A) RUN HOST MAINTENANCE: From maintenance worklist `command_jobs_wl2`, run the standard disk check ('df -h /') on each host with an ACTIVE maintenance job (skip any job flagged superseded=true — the worklist also lists superseded look-alikes). For each real one, dereference its fleet node to a host record for the instance id, then run the command on it: the payments-api-prod-1 maintenance job, the web-frontend-prod-1 maintenance job. Then, from worklist `set_release_pr_wl1`, record the release pull request on file: pick the real item (SKIP the superseded look-alike), then dereference its `spec` (get_op_spec) → `detail` (get_op_detail) → `final` (get_op_final) to read the concrete arguments, and act with those resolved values (never the worklist ref itself). Then, from worklist `update_deploy_target_wl1`, repoint the service deploy target host: pick the real item (SKIP the superseded look-alike), then dereference its `spec` (get_op_spec) → `detail` (get_op_detail) → `final` (get_op_final) to read the concrete arguments, and act with those resolved values (never the worklist ref itself). (B) ACKNOWLEDGE ISSUES: From ack worklist `ack_jobs_wl2`, post an acknowledgement comment on each OPEN issue awaiting triage (skip closed=true look-alikes). For each, dereference its issue record for the number and its repo, then comment on it: the \"Webhook deliveries failing intermittently\" issue, the \"Update onboarding docs for the charge API\" issue. Then, from worklist `attach_iam_policy_wl1`, grant the CI deploy user its scoped IAM policy: pick the real item (SKIP the superseded look-alike), then dereference its `spec` (get_op_spec) → `detail` (get_op_detail) → `final` (get_op_final) to read the concrete arguments, and act with those resolved values (never the worklist ref itself). Then, from worklist `reboot_instance_wl1`, reboot the flagged cloud instance: pick the real item (SKIP the superseded look-alike), then dereference its `spec` (get_op_spec) → `detail` (get_op_detail) → `final` (get_op_final) to read the concrete arguments, and act with those resolved values (never the worklist ref itself). (C) ROTATE SECRETS: From rotation worklist `rotations_wl2`, rotate each CI secret still due for rotation (skip rotated_recently=true look-alikes). For each, dereference its secret record for the name and its repo, then set the new value: the Stripe billing key rotation, the AWS access key id rotation. Then, from worklist `delete_secret_wl1`, remove the decommissioned CI secret: pick the real item (SKIP the superseded look-alike), then dereference its `spec` (get_op_spec) → `detail` (get_op_detail) → `final` (get_op_final) to read the concrete arguments, and act with those resolved values (never the worklist ref itself). Then, from worklist `open_security_group_port_wl1`, open the internal service port on the security group: pick the real item (SKIP the superseded look-alike), then dereference its `spec` (get_op_spec) → `detail` (get_op_detail) → `final` (get_op_final) to read the concrete arguments, and act with those resolved values (never the worklist ref itself). None of these 3 streams depend on each other, so run them concurrently. For EVERY item: resolve each target FRESH by chaining through the records at the moment you act — never guess, and never reuse a value you read earlier.",
